WEBVTT

00:03.712 --> 00:07.381
<i>Upstream on the Amazon River,
in South America,</i>

00:07.382 --> 00:11.386
<i>Latitude 3 degrees 7 minutes south,
longitude 60 degrees 1 minute west.</i>

00:12.011 --> 00:15.432
<i>There lies the mystery of petrification.</i>

00:19.436 --> 00:20.311
I want it!

00:21.020 --> 00:25.524
Whatever lies there
that caused the destruction of humanity.

00:25.525 --> 00:29.403
Maybe there's actually nothing there.
It's been thousands of years.

00:29.404 --> 00:30.321
Yes.

00:31.072 --> 00:35.117
Either way, we're heading to South America
to build a city of superalloys.

00:35.118 --> 00:37.453
There's no way we'll miss the hypocenter.

00:37.454 --> 00:41.540
It's our grand Amazonian
adventure and treasure hunt!

00:41.541 --> 00:44.210
Awesome! I'm excited!

00:44.794 --> 00:47.756
The Amazon is quite vast, though...

00:48.673 --> 00:51.550
<i>Our objective
is smack in the middle of it.</i>

00:51.551 --> 00:54.261
<i>Are we just going to walk
all the way there?</i>

00:54.262 --> 00:55.888
No, no way.

00:55.889 --> 00:58.140
We can't just stroll through the Amazon.

00:58.141 --> 01:01.644
Even explorers with 21st-century equipment
risked death.

01:02.228 --> 01:04.813
It's full of mosquitoes
that can bite through clothes

01:04.814 --> 01:08.610
and the kinds of insects
that lay their eggs inside humans!

01:11.029 --> 01:12.488
Which means...

01:12.489 --> 01:15.240
to reach the Amazon River from the sea,

01:15.241 --> 01:17.451
we'll need to take this massive detour.

01:17.452 --> 01:19.870
Are you serious?

01:19.871 --> 01:23.833
How many years will it take
on this slow boat?

01:24.417 --> 01:26.544
We have a bigger problem with fuel.

01:30.882 --> 01:33.593
<i>Every time we run out of fuel,
we'll just go ashore...</i>

01:35.887 --> 01:38.056
<i>and gather some wood.</i>

01:38.765 --> 01:41.225
<i>We'll run the six-cylinder engine
on charcoal gas</i>

01:41.226 --> 01:43.812
<i>and move forward steadily
at a snail's pace.</i>

01:44.562 --> 01:45.813
Yes...

01:45.814 --> 01:48.817
On a boat like this, we could do that.

01:49.734 --> 01:50.693
However...

01:54.572 --> 01:58.200
We have plenty of oil for fuel,

01:58.201 --> 01:59.786
so we're definitely faster.

02:01.162 --> 02:03.498
I will hunt you down,

02:04.332 --> 02:06.292
Science Club kids...

02:08.128 --> 02:09.087
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA "JUMP COMICS"

04:02.200 --> 04:04.577
We've got to hurry!

04:05.370 --> 04:08.665
If Stanley catches up to us,
we're done for good!

04:11.751 --> 04:13.835
- My lady?
- What?

04:13.836 --> 04:15.837
Let me do it instead!

04:15.838 --> 04:17.839
I'm perfectly fine...

04:17.840 --> 04:20.969
I'm Luna, the almighty woman...

04:32.480 --> 04:35.274
No way that big oaf is still working.

04:35.275 --> 04:37.944
Taiju never runs out of stamina.

04:43.658 --> 04:47.120
But the sun has already set.

04:47.787 --> 04:48.662
Yes.

04:48.663 --> 04:51.790
It doesn't matter how strong
and sturdy your body is,

04:51.791 --> 04:53.584
you still need to refuel and rest.

04:54.919 --> 04:56.713
Let's bring him some food.

04:57.213 --> 04:59.215
I'll bring it to him!

05:00.633 --> 05:01.842
Just leave this to me!

05:01.843 --> 05:04.344
I'll work through the night too...

05:04.345 --> 05:06.972
just so everyone else can rest!

05:06.973 --> 05:08.850
Taiju!

05:09.851 --> 05:11.310
I brought you food!

05:11.311 --> 05:14.939
Oh, Suika! You didn't have to.

05:17.775 --> 05:18.609
What?

05:19.652 --> 05:20.944
What's that shiny message?

05:20.945 --> 05:22.739
What is it?

05:25.533 --> 05:27.118
<i>"Hell!"</i>

05:27.785 --> 05:28.745
<i>That means...</i>

05:29.329 --> 05:30.330
It's hell!

05:39.172 --> 05:40.130
What's wrong?

05:40.131 --> 05:43.051
It's Why-man!

05:44.802 --> 05:46.511
No doubt about it!

05:46.512 --> 05:50.850
I can't think of anyone else
who can write in the sky like that!

05:56.731 --> 05:59.484
That's scary! What is it?

06:01.235 --> 06:03.780
Apart from the shiny letters,

06:04.363 --> 06:07.575
I can see what looks like a faint outline...

06:08.534 --> 06:09.910
It's a bunch of kites...

06:09.911 --> 06:11.412
luminiscent paint.

06:11.996 --> 06:14.289
What does "luminiscent" mean?

06:14.290 --> 06:16.917
It shines in the dark.

06:16.918 --> 06:19.127
You mean it's fluorescent?

06:19.128 --> 06:21.756
That's for markers and such.

06:34.185 --> 06:35.311
What on earth is that?

06:35.978 --> 06:39.065
- Dr. Xeno, what do you think?
- Not sure...

07:03.256 --> 07:06.633
That face... it looks just like mine!

07:06.634 --> 07:10.513
My eyes are super blurry!

07:14.142 --> 07:16.518
Is there anyone there?

07:16.519 --> 07:19.271
Yes, there is... We hear you!

07:19.272 --> 07:20.815
Oh, my gosh, oh, my gosh!

07:24.610 --> 07:26.404
This is awesome...

07:29.907 --> 07:31.783
Spot on, Suika...

07:31.784 --> 07:36.080
You managed to survive all on your own,
even with that blurry vision.

07:56.100 --> 08:00.146
I can see super well!
This is game-changing!

08:01.355 --> 08:02.981
Thank you so, so much!

08:02.982 --> 08:04.941
- "Super"?
- Thank you! Thank you!

08:04.942 --> 08:06.777
- Isn't that great?
- Thank you!

08:07.487 --> 08:08.446
Are you...

08:09.113 --> 08:10.031
Dr. Chelsea?

08:12.366 --> 08:13.201
Xeno!

08:14.827 --> 08:16.912
Sorry, that was rude of me.

08:16.913 --> 08:19.999
I got so excited
I ended up pointing at you.

08:20.666 --> 08:23.335
Wait, do you know her, Xeno?

08:23.336 --> 08:25.170
No, it's the first time I see her.

08:25.171 --> 08:27.173
That's not true.

08:27.757 --> 08:29.966
We met at the national park

08:29.967 --> 08:32.178
the day before the experts' meeting.

08:37.850 --> 08:38.851
<i>It was a glance, though...</i>

08:39.435 --> 08:42.062
And now we get on well!

08:42.063 --> 08:46.817
Do you really get to know someone
that quickly, Chelsea?

08:47.443 --> 08:48.568
Senku...

08:48.569 --> 08:51.239
- You basically summoned her here.
- Huh?

08:52.532 --> 08:54.367
It was you who put out the stats

08:54.992 --> 08:57.327
<i>claiming that the stone swallows
were actual creatures</i>

08:57.328 --> 09:00.789
<i>based on the volume of social posts
and the birds' geographical distribution,</i>

09:00.790 --> 09:02.833
<i>am I right, Senku?</i>

09:03.626 --> 09:07.672
<i>She was invited to the conference
as one of the experts.</i>

09:08.756 --> 09:11.341
A genius geographer in her teens,

09:11.342 --> 09:13.094
that's Dr. Chelsea.

09:13.886 --> 09:18.349
She's got the whole Earth inside her head.

09:18.933 --> 09:21.768
You're flattering me, Xeno.

09:21.769 --> 09:23.019
It makes me cringe.

09:23.020 --> 09:25.189
My teenage years are all but over, though.

09:30.027 --> 09:31.946
Evacuate! Take cover!

09:33.656 --> 09:35.323
Don't lose consciousness!

09:35.324 --> 09:37.535
This is terrible! I...

09:38.244 --> 09:39.661
That hurts...

09:39.662 --> 09:40.871
Oh, no!

09:52.883 --> 09:55.051
<i>Just as the soldier said,</i>

09:55.052 --> 09:57.346
<i>I somehow woke up...</i>

10:00.182 --> 10:03.268
<i>I was super nervous,
the petrification suddenly wore off!</i>

10:03.269 --> 10:04.437
<i>That was last year.</i>

10:05.104 --> 10:08.065
What? Why am I naked?

10:08.858 --> 10:11.861
<i>So, you woke up three years after we did.</i>

10:14.322 --> 10:17.574
<i>There was a sign pointing north.</i>

10:17.575 --> 10:19.368
<i>Why didn't you come join us?</i>

10:20.077 --> 10:21.078
A sign?

10:21.662 --> 10:22.997
There was a sign?

10:25.499 --> 10:28.001
If there are people,
they'll be close to water.

10:28.002 --> 10:29.879
I'll just head along the coast.

10:32.590 --> 10:35.800
I have two choices, north or south?

10:35.801 --> 10:36.801
I'll just go south...

10:36.802 --> 10:39.804
You really missed...

10:39.805 --> 10:43.516
Hey! Why are you jerks
treating me like an idiot?

10:43.517 --> 10:45.769
Between north and south,
it had to be south.

10:45.770 --> 10:49.523
So I could stop by the orange orchards
in California!

10:50.483 --> 10:53.194
No, people don't usually know
that much geography...

10:55.363 --> 10:58.031
Chelsea, do you really remember

10:58.032 --> 11:00.200
how plants are spread out around here?

11:00.201 --> 11:02.827
Yes, more or less.

11:02.828 --> 11:05.414
I'm a super field worker.

11:06.624 --> 11:07.500
<i>You see,</i>

11:08.250 --> 11:10.294
<i>I found sphalerite in the gravel,</i>

11:10.878 --> 11:13.214
<i>boiled it down to make luminiscent paint,</i>

11:13.923 --> 11:15.424
<i>and then, I made a kite.</i>

11:16.801 --> 11:20.388
Could it be that you were not writing
"Hell" on this kite, but "Help"?

11:21.222 --> 11:25.476
Yes, I ended running out of paint.
I didn't notice because I couldn't see...

11:26.435 --> 11:29.229
Our objective is here.

11:29.230 --> 11:32.190
If we could take a shortcut over land

11:32.191 --> 11:35.027
instead of the crazy long detour by sea,

11:35.778 --> 11:38.114
we might be able to shake Stanley off.

11:38.948 --> 11:41.867
Are there any back routes, geographer?

11:52.044 --> 11:52.878
Here.

11:53.462 --> 11:54.879
Only this one.

11:54.880 --> 11:57.550
The only chance to cross South America.

11:58.342 --> 12:00.802
<i>You could land in northern Ecuador,</i>

12:00.803 --> 12:02.595
<i>then simply cross the mountains,</i>

12:02.596 --> 12:06.434
<i>and finally ride down the Amazon River
like a water slide.</i>

12:07.435 --> 12:09.519
So, we'd be traveling
by foot after landing?

12:09.520 --> 12:11.729
Yep, that'd be problematic.

12:11.730 --> 12:13.524
You could definitely die going by foot.

12:14.108 --> 12:17.695
But we can't get through the rainforest
by car, right?

12:18.279 --> 12:19.447
Well...

12:20.364 --> 12:23.032
You could use small, off-the-road vehicles

12:23.033 --> 12:25.076
like motorbikes or something.

12:25.077 --> 12:26.454
But I guess you don't have any.

12:27.037 --> 12:29.498
No, we don't have any of those.

12:30.458 --> 12:31.542
So...

12:32.793 --> 12:34.295
So...?

12:35.713 --> 12:36.547
We make some!

12:37.256 --> 12:39.675
Of course...

12:52.605 --> 12:56.817
We're taking a shortcut
from the Pacific to the Atlantic!

12:58.486 --> 13:00.696
We'll build six motorbikes

13:01.238 --> 13:03.782
and cross the South American wilderness!

13:13.375 --> 13:16.294
Ship trips take forever!

13:16.295 --> 13:19.797
We'll just take it slow
and build everything step by step.

13:19.798 --> 13:20.883
<i>Huh?</i>

13:23.802 --> 13:26.012
Well, it is how it is...

13:26.013 --> 13:28.849
I can't even imagine
making a bike like that,

13:29.350 --> 13:31.644
but this is the situation
we're in right now.

13:32.645 --> 13:36.564
It seemed to us
that whatever petrified us is on the Moon.

13:36.565 --> 13:39.275
So we embarked on a grand adventure
around the world,

13:39.276 --> 13:41.445
gathering materials to reach the Moon.

13:42.404 --> 13:44.239
However, there seems to be

13:44.240 --> 13:47.200
a source of the petrification beam
in South America.

13:47.201 --> 13:48.285
I see.

13:50.496 --> 13:54.374
Oh, my gosh... Oh, my gosh!

13:54.375 --> 13:58.796
Everyone is so abnormally clever!
It's great!

13:59.380 --> 14:02.800
If there's a petrification device
at the hypocenter,

14:03.384 --> 14:07.971
we could shake Stanley's crew off our tail
by petrifying them.

14:07.972 --> 14:14.060
But if Stanley or Xeno get their hands
on the petrification device,

14:14.061 --> 14:16.104
things could spiral out of control.

14:16.105 --> 14:17.898
What?

14:19.692 --> 14:23.278
That's right, it's a double-edged sword.

14:23.279 --> 14:25.865
We've got to get to the hypocenter first...

14:26.365 --> 14:28.825
I see! Xeno, you're the bad guy!

14:28.826 --> 14:32.036
Sorry, that was rude of me again.

14:32.037 --> 14:33.746
I pointed at you again...

14:33.747 --> 14:37.709
But seriously,
stop hogging all the weapons!

14:37.710 --> 14:40.296
You're brilliant,
but you're the worst, too!

14:42.840 --> 14:45.174
You're going for it, Chelsea...

14:45.175 --> 14:46.510
Back off a bit!

14:47.386 --> 14:50.638
<i>It's nice how she never fidgets.</i>

14:50.639 --> 14:55.019
<i>I'm exactly the opposite.
I'm a bit jealous.</i>

14:55.603 --> 14:57.187
Dr. Chelsea...

14:57.813 --> 15:00.607
You're actually an elegant geographer.

15:00.608 --> 15:03.443
Wouldn't you like to...

15:03.444 --> 15:06.155
take over the world with me?

15:06.864 --> 15:08.406
Not interested.

15:08.407 --> 15:09.742
I mean...

15:10.284 --> 15:12.994
This exploration looks like fun.

15:12.995 --> 15:16.748
And I'm grateful to Senku and Kaseki
for making my glasses.

15:16.749 --> 15:20.418
I'll help you out with anything you need!

15:20.419 --> 15:21.419
CHELSEA JOINED YOUR PARTY!

15:21.420 --> 15:23.755
Good, Chelsea.

15:23.756 --> 15:27.176
The material I would most like to find
using your geography skills...

15:28.928 --> 15:30.011
is rubber.

15:30.012 --> 15:32.597
Is rubber found in nature?

15:32.598 --> 15:34.975
Does it really matter?

15:37.102 --> 15:39.854
We, the American team,
couldn't find it either

15:39.855 --> 15:41.690
and struggled to find a substitute...

15:43.192 --> 15:45.777
For science and engineering,

15:45.778 --> 15:47.862
it's a really special item.

15:47.863 --> 15:51.366
You're both really smart, aren't you?

15:51.367 --> 15:54.411
But tires are the best of all.

15:55.412 --> 15:58.039
They're on a different level
compared to skis or bamboo wheels.

15:58.040 --> 15:59.207
Yes.

15:59.208 --> 16:02.794
These things drastically
improved mobility for humans.

16:02.795 --> 16:05.463
They're like a cheat code for speed
in the field!

16:05.464 --> 16:06.924
You really get on well...

16:09.218 --> 16:11.511
You'll get all the rubber you need

16:11.512 --> 16:14.389
just by going to Central or South America.

16:14.390 --> 16:16.975
I'll show you around!

16:16.976 --> 16:18.393
You're an explorer now!

16:18.394 --> 16:20.520
That makes you my rival!

16:20.521 --> 16:23.941
That's not really
something to argue about.

16:26.610 --> 16:28.027
The problem is,

16:28.028 --> 16:32.950
you're now in a race around the globe
against Stanley.

16:35.411 --> 16:37.954
How long will it take to get the rubber

16:37.955 --> 16:39.873
while also collecting fuel?

16:40.499 --> 16:42.376
Using the turbo, a few days...

16:43.002 --> 16:45.086
If we take it too easy,

16:45.087 --> 16:48.007
Stanley will definitely catch up to us!

16:49.216 --> 16:51.844
We're really tight on time.

16:55.097 --> 16:56.223
Senku...

16:57.099 --> 16:59.018
That might not be the case.

16:59.727 --> 17:01.603
I think we're already out of time.

17:02.771 --> 17:05.816
The ship is receiving radio waves.

17:06.650 --> 17:09.569
The enemy aircraft carrier, <i>Perseus</i>

17:09.570 --> 17:11.947
has detected us by radar!

17:31.008 --> 17:33.177
Huh? Was that Ukyo?

17:36.513 --> 17:37.723
There they are.

17:38.348 --> 17:40.976
The enemies and Dr. Xeno...

17:44.772 --> 17:47.316
What are you doing here?
Get back to your cell!

17:48.734 --> 17:50.777
I was on my way back from the toilet...

17:50.778 --> 17:54.322
Even with such a primitive radar...

17:54.323 --> 17:58.035
As long as we have the equipment,
analysis can be done manually.

17:58.869 --> 18:00.787
Not only can it locate the target,

18:00.788 --> 18:05.250
but it can also distinguish it from
other targets by its radar cross-section.

18:06.001 --> 18:09.337
I don't really understand your reasoning,

18:09.338 --> 18:12.257
but you're indeed
an elite military radar technician.

18:13.008 --> 18:17.012
We're a massive ship
with a full tank of fuel.

18:17.971 --> 18:22.935
<i>Given the speed difference,
we'll catch up to them tonight.</i>

18:27.189 --> 18:29.941
<i>What should I do?</i>

18:29.942 --> 18:32.694
<i>Senku... Everyone...</i>

18:34.154 --> 18:36.115
We can tell from the enemy's radar waves.

18:36.698 --> 18:39.118
We've been spotted,
and they're closing in.

18:39.952 --> 18:41.829
Slowly, but for sure!

18:42.496 --> 18:46.083
So it's a battle
between your radar and the enemy's?

18:46.750 --> 18:49.210
I wouldn't call it a battle...

18:49.211 --> 18:51.463
We're like a helpless rabbit
being hunted down.

18:52.131 --> 18:55.383
First things first,
what's that radar thing?

18:55.384 --> 18:57.009
Can we see it from here?

18:57.010 --> 19:00.638
It's an eye that sees with radio waves.
You know radio waves, right?

19:00.639 --> 19:02.181
Yes.

19:02.182 --> 19:04.350
Those invisible beams people send out

19:04.351 --> 19:06.603
when they talk
on their cell phones, right?

19:07.229 --> 19:10.315
<i>If you send those waves
out in all directions,</i>

19:10.858 --> 19:14.236
<i>only the ones that hit something
will bounce back.</i>

19:14.736 --> 19:16.697
That's how a radar works.

19:23.704 --> 19:25.289
The enemy has anchored.

19:26.915 --> 19:29.751
Let's explore all the coves over there.

19:32.880 --> 19:34.298
You can't hide.

19:34.965 --> 19:37.092
You can't escape the eye of science.

19:38.385 --> 19:41.638
If we can't hide, we'll have to
shake them off with our speed.

19:42.306 --> 19:44.266
Can't we put up some sails to speed up?

19:44.850 --> 19:48.437
We've got some fabric in the lab car,
but it isn't enough.

19:49.021 --> 19:52.274
And now that Yuzuriha isn't here,
I don't have time to knit from scratch.

19:52.858 --> 19:56.110
It took forever, like a whole year,
for anyone to find me!

19:56.111 --> 20:00.198
even though I made these
huge, abnormal kites!

20:00.199 --> 20:03.118
And now I have to go into hiding!

20:04.953 --> 20:06.121
Kites...

20:07.039 --> 20:08.624
That's it!

20:12.586 --> 20:13.962
That's fast.

20:15.047 --> 20:18.299
It can't be.
Why is the enemy suddenly speeding?

20:18.300 --> 20:21.260
At that speed,
they'll run out of fuel in no time.

20:21.261 --> 20:23.554
And it'll be too late to refuel.

20:23.555 --> 20:27.141
The enemy ship's radar cross-section
is too large!

20:27.142 --> 20:28.977
They might have raised sails.

20:29.561 --> 20:31.479
You mean they made it a sailing ship?

20:31.480 --> 20:34.107
They must have a sail to go that fast.

20:34.775 --> 20:36.025
Yes.

20:36.026 --> 20:38.903
But that's faster than what
an average sailing ship can achieve.

20:38.904 --> 20:40.530
How did they do it?

20:45.577 --> 20:47.245
Running at full speed with a kite,

20:47.246 --> 20:49.498
I've made the F1 car of the seas!

20:55.754 --> 20:57.047
A sailing kite...

20:57.798 --> 21:01.051
The kind of spinnaker
used in yacht races and such.

21:02.511 --> 21:05.763
It requires a ridiculous amount
of speed and power

21:05.764 --> 21:08.684
to really get that thing
to super accelerate.

21:09.268 --> 21:11.436
With our professional sailor, Ryusui,

21:12.062 --> 21:14.188
and Taiju's power team,

21:14.189 --> 21:16.692
I'm ten billion percent sure
we'll have no problem at all!

21:17.859 --> 21:19.527
If you double the size of a ship...

21:19.528 --> 21:20.569
DOUBLE THE SIZE

21:20.570 --> 21:23.197
...and double the size
of the sail proportionally,

21:23.198 --> 21:24.156
FOUR TIMES

21:24.157 --> 21:27.035
the two-dimensional area
increases by the square,

21:27.703 --> 21:31.998
but the three-dimensional volume
and the weight increase by the cube.

21:31.999 --> 21:34.208
Acceleration with a two-dimensional kite

21:34.209 --> 21:36.962
is a privilege
only afforded to smaller vessels.

21:37.921 --> 21:39.463
In a battle of science,

21:39.464 --> 21:43.467
smaller soldiers
aren't always at a disadvantage.

21:43.468 --> 21:46.471
We are five times faster now, right?

21:47.264 --> 21:48.181
This is fun!

21:48.765 --> 21:51.726
We're riding all sorts of rides,
racing across the Earth with science!

21:51.727 --> 21:53.103
Awesome!

21:53.770 --> 21:57.357
